  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nMy previous discussion from earlier this week was talking about the \nSummer-like temperatures and this discussion will focus on our \npotential first freeze of the Fall season. It appears to be a tale \nof two seasons. The large upper-level low pressure that brought us \nthe much colder temperatures continues to slowly move off to the \nnortheast. On the backside of the low, a tight pressure gradient is \nresulting in gusty north winds late this morning into this \nafternoon. Due to continued cold-air advection, temperatures \nstruggle to warm into the 50s with a wind chill in the 40s. \nThankfully the winds do not linger. Surface high pressure begins to \nsettle into the area this evening, causing winds to become light in \nresponse.\n\nWith the center of the surface high pressure over the region \nSaturday morning and clear skies, our coldest temperatures so far \nthis season develop. Widespread near and below-freezing temperatures \nare anticipated with a low chance (10-20%) a few localized areas \nreaching a hard freeze (28 degrees or less). Due to these \ntemperatures, the entire area is under a Freeze Warning from late \ntonight into Saturday morning. Outside of the temperatures, the \nlight winds should allow for frost development. In addition, enough \nlingering moisture may result in patchy, shallow fog. While \nvisibility reductions should be minor, this fog could cause a thin \nglaze of ice to form on elevated surface, especially bridges and \noverpasses. That said, this threat is low (20-40% chance), but use \ncaution on your morning drive. \n\nThe cold weather remains short-lived as upper-level ridging resumes \nSaturday afternoon with increasing southwest winds. The increasing \nupper-level heights and warm southwest winds are forecast to help \npush temperatures back into the upper 50s to near 60, despite the \ncold start. This warming trend will continue into next week with dry \nconditions continuing. High temperatures climbing back into the 60s \non Sunday, push 70 by Tuesday, and well into the 70s by mid-week.\n\nWhile our weather remains mostly quiet, an active pattern begins to \ntake shape for the CONUS late next week. The previously mentioned \nupper-level ridge will slide east as long-wave troughing returns to \nthe western CONUS. Within this troughing, several weaker shortwaves \nlift northeast throughout the week. These may result in a low chance \nof rain. Our next best chance of rain is not set to arrive until \nlate in the week as another upper-level low pressure takes shape to \nour southwest. Showers and thunderstorms may be possible as this \nsystem approaches the region.",
